          An asphalt millings driveway is usually worth it if you want a firmer, less dusty surface than gravel without paying for new asphalt: installed costs typically run roughly $2 to $5 per square foot, against roughly $4 to $10 for new asphalt. Millings are recycled road asphalt, and when they are spread on a good base and compacted well, the old binder slowly glues them into a semi-solid surface over the first warm season. The trade-offs are a rough look, loose material and a tar smell early on, varying supplier quality, and local rules in some areas about where recycled asphalt can go.
        </p>

        <div id="what" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Are Asphalt Millings, Exactly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            When a road or parking lot is resurfaced, crews often grind off the top layer of worn asphalt with a milling machine before laying new pavement. What comes off is a mix of crushed stone coated in aged asphalt binder, broken into pieces that range from fine grit to chunks an inch or so across. In the industry it is called reclaimed asphalt pavement, or RAP. Homeowners usually hear it called millings, recycled asphalt, crushed asphalt or grindings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most RAP goes back into new asphalt at the plant, which is why it is one of the most recycled materials in the country. Surplus millings are sold, and sometimes given away, by paving contractors, asphalt plants and some public works departments. That is what ends up on driveways, farm lanes, parking pads and rural roads.
          </p>

          <p>
            What makes millings different from gravel is the binder. Asphalt pavement is mostly stone held together by a few percent of asphalt cement by weight. After years on a road that binder is oxidized and stiff, but it is still there. When millings are compacted and warmed by summer sun, the binder on neighboring pieces softens slightly and sticks them together. Over time, a well-compacted millings driveway firms up into something between loose gravel and real pavement.
          </p>

          <p>
            It does not become new asphalt. It never has the smooth, dense, sealed surface of hot-mix pavement laid by a paver and rolled at temperature. Expect a textured, gray-black surface that is firm underfoot and under tires once it sets, sheds some loose pieces at the edges, and needs occasional patching. If you understand it as better gravel rather than cheap asphalt, you will be happy with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Does an Asphalt Millings Driveway Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Millings are priced like aggregate, by the ton or the cubic yard, and the price depends almost entirely on local supply. Near an active paving market you may find them for roughly $10 to $40 per ton, sometimes less, sometimes free if a contractor needs to get rid of a pile. In areas with little road work, or where plants reuse all their RAP, they can cost as much as crushed stone or be hard to find at all. Prices change from year to year, so call local paving contractors and asphalt plants for current numbers.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
