        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An asphalt millings driveway is a sensible middle ground: typically roughly $2 to $5 per square foot installed, firmer and less dusty than gravel once it binds, and far cheaper than new asphalt. The results depend on a solid base, heavy compaction in lifts, and warm-weather installation that lets the old binder do its job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Go in with realistic expectations. Millings look like rough old asphalt, stay loose and smelly for a while, vary by source, and are regulated in some places. Check local rules, inspect the pile, insist on a roller, and wait until the surface has firmed up before you think about sealing it.
          </p>
